+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits — Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Field tablets can be offline for up to 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The record store must define what happens when edits derived from different starting states of the same visit arrive: which edit becomes the current record, and what a coordinator must review. The spreadsheet process this project replaces resolves the same situation by overwriting — the quiet loss of a submitted correction is the problem the HLD names.
+
+Two edits to the same visit may touch unrelated fields (an observer-name correction and a missing species) or scientifically related ones (a species-identification revision and the cover estimate taken under the old identification). Which edits interact is botanical judgment: the server can detect that two submissions overlap a field, but not that two disjoint edits interact scientifically. This decision selects the concurrency policy; tablet-sync and review-export are sized around its outcome.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Gate (inherited):
+
+- **No submitted edit is silently discarded** (HLD tenet). A policy that overwrites a submission's effect without retention and without review is eliminated.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **No unreviewed combination of scientifically related edits** (major). A policy that combines concurrent edits automatically must judge which fields are safe to combine — and the project's Non-Goals exclude inferring scientific intent.
+- **Review volume within two coordinators' weekly capacity** (major). Two coordinators clear review work ahead of a Friday export (HLD tenet: workflows two coordinators can operate without a systems administrator).
+- **Provenance inspectability** (major; HLD tenet: the path from a submitted observation to an exported value stays inspectable). Simpler provenance chains are stronger.
+- **Maintenance burden** (moderate). Rules that must be kept correct as the visit schema evolves carry ongoing engineering cost the team does not have staffed for.
+- **Correction latency for field workers** (moderate). How long an accepted edit waits before it is the current record.
+- **Export completeness** (moderate). How often the Friday CSV must omit visits pending review.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Last-write-wins overwrite
+
+The most recently received submission replaces the current record; earlier submissions are superseded. This is the spreadsheet status quo.
+
+- Gate: **eliminated** — overwriting discards a submitted edit's effect with no retention and no review.
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every submission is a complete visit payload naming the base revision it was edited from. An edit based on the current revision advances the record; an edit based on any other state is retained as a competing revision, and a coordinator selects which complete revision becomes current. The server never combines content from two submissions.
+
+Demonstration: Visit V is at revision 3. Worker A corrects the observer list (base 3) and is accepted as revision 4. Worker B, offline since revision 3, submits a species fix (base 3) — retained as a competing revision; V becomes unresolved. A coordinator compares B's complete visit against revision 4, selects one; the outcome cites both submissions, and the other stays retained.
+
+- Gate: **passes** — every submission is retained with author, receipt time, and base revision, regardless of outcome.
+- No unreviewed combination: **strong** — nothing is ever combined; every competing edit, related or not, reaches a coordinator.
+- Review volume: **weak** — every concurrent edit, however unrelated its fields, creates review work.
+- Provenance: **strong** — every current revision is one submitter's complete payload; the chain from exported value to submission is one line.
+- Maintenance: **strong** — no merge rules; a small fixed state machine.
+- Correction latency: **partial** — clean edits apply on acceptance; any edit colliding with an open conflict waits for resolution.
+- Export completeness: **weak** — unresolved visits are held out, including visits whose conflicts are trivially unrelated.
+
+Summary: trades review volume and export completeness for never auto-combining and near-zero rule maintenance.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission carries its base state and structured field changes; the server merges three-way. Disjoint field changes combine automatically into the current record; overlapping changes queue for review. At its best this respects both workers' effort: an observer-name fix and a species addition from different tablets both land without coordinator involvement, and review sees only genuine conflicts.
+
+Demonstration of the failure it must not have: Worker A reidentifies species X (*Spartina* → *Distichlis*); Worker B, working from the same base state, corrects X's cover estimate. Name and cover are disjoint fields, so the rules combine them: the record pairs A's new identification with B's cover estimate taken under the old identification — a record no worker observed, with no signal to anyone. Catching this requires the rules to encode that a cover estimate belongs to its identification — one instance of the per-field-type knowledge (scalars, observer lists, species entries) the rule set must be maintained to carry.
+
+- Gate: **passes** — original submissions are retained; merging displaces effects, not records.
+- No unreviewed combination: **weak** — auto-combination is the mechanism; disjoint-field rules cannot see scientific relatedness across fields.
+- Review volume: **strong** — only overlapping edits queue.
+- Provenance: **partial** — current records are composites; the chain from an exported value to a single submitted visit must be reconstructed field by field, which review-export must then surface.
+- Maintenance: **weak** — per-field-type merge rules, kept correct as the visit schema evolves.
+- Correction latency: **strong** — disjoint edits apply on acceptance.
+- Export completeness: **strong** — fewer visits sit unresolved.
+
+Summary: trades rule maintenance and unreviewed combinations for minimal review volume.
+
+### Approval-gated merge
+
+Field-aware merge with one correction to the above: every auto-combined record is held for coordinator approval before it becomes current. This answers the silent-combination objection directly — a human sees every combination — while review becomes confirmation of a proposed merged record rather than comparison of competing ones.
+
+- Gate: **passes**.
+- No unreviewed combination: **partial** — combinations are seen before they land, though each is a field-level diff rather than a whole visit, and the rules' relatedness judgments are embedded in what the coordinator is shown.
+- Review volume: **weak** — every concurrent edit still produces review work (an approval per combination plus a comparison per overlap), in field-level detail.
+- Provenance: **partial** — composite records, as above.
+- Maintenance: **weak** — the full rule set is still required.
+- Correction latency: **partial** — combined edits wait for approval.
+- Export completeness: **partial** — combined records are held until approved.
+
+Summary: retains merge's rule maintenance while restoring most of its review volume — it pays both costs.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The criteria merge wins on — review volume, correction latency, export completeness — all presuppose that the merge rules combine only edits that are safe to combine. Producing that presupposition requires exactly the judgment the project excludes (Non-Goal: no inference of scientific intent): rules that cannot tell an identification fix from a cover estimate taken under the old identification. The team weighed a larger review queue against maintained rules that could silently produce scientifically wrong records, and chose the queue. The decision turns on the no-silent-discard tenet at the gate and on refusing unreviewed inference of scientific intent among the major criteria; it accepts a real cost against the review-volume, latency, and completeness criteria, held within bounds by the two-coordinator operability tenet — the queue must stay clearable weekly, which obligates watching it (below). The approval-gated variant is not a middle path: it keeps the rules and most of the queue, paying both costs.
+
+Committing to whole-visit revisions forecloses automatic application of any concurrent edit: every collision, however trivially unrelated its fields, costs coordinator time and can hold a visit out of a Friday export.
+
+What the two downstream designs must accommodate:
+
+- **Tablet-sync**: retain each visit's current revision number as the edit base; submit complete payloads (no field deltas, no base snapshots); treat "retained as competing revision" as a visible server outcome the worker can see and understand, not an error; expect that a worker's edit may sit pending for days when it collides.
+- **Review-export**: a comparison screen presenting competing complete revisions against the current revision; hold unresolved visits out of the CSV (its own eligibility rule, keyed to record-store's state); surface review-queue depth, because a chronically growing queue is the signal that this decision should be re-run — and expect Friday exports that legitimately omit unresolved visits, with the omissions visible to the science partner rather than hidden.
+
+Re-running this decision: field-aware merge (with or without the approval gate) becomes the right choice if (1) review volume demonstrably exceeds what two coordinators can clear weekly — a queue that grows across weeks and presses against the Friday deadline — and (2) the team can maintain per-field-type merge rules with scientific oversight, so rule changes are reviewable by the coordinators rather than implicit in code. A narrower move short of reversal: an explicitly safe field pair (e.g., observer-list corrections) could later be merged with coordinator approval as a carve-out, without reopening the whole policy.
